window.MANUAL_SEARCH = [{"title": "EU5 Population Editor manual", "label": "Introduction", "url": "index.html", "text": "EU5 Population Editor is an offline desktop tool for inspecting and editing Europa Universalis V population data. Browse countries and editable locations, review exact changes, and export a separate mod. Inspect Compare the original game data with your confirmed work. Review Preview a draft before changing your project. Apply Confirm the proposal, save the project, then export a mod. The 1.0.3 editor shows Alpha's population of 123,450 people using synthetic demonstration data. The image follows the manual theme. Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ Screenshots in this edition show the actual application with synthetic demonstration data: fictional countries and locations, and an illustrative map. They do not contain shipped EU5 game assets. Your installed game supplies the data and geography when you use the editor. Start here Install the editor Choose the Windows portable, Linux portable or Debian package. Create your first project Load your game, select a location and make a reviewed edit. Update an existing project Compare old and new game data before carrying changes forward. What you can edit Population groups are identified by population type, culture and religion. You can change a group's size, add or remove groups using the game's definitions, rebalance demographic proportions, and scale a location's total. The editor also provides location browsing, an optional interactive map, validation and change reports. The editor changes population setup data through exported mods. It is not a save-game editor, ownership editor or full mod-development environment. Your installed game files remain read-only. Three states, one deliberate workflow State Meaning Saved and exported? Original The loaded game data Retained as the comparison baseline Working Your confirmed edits Yes Draft A pending proposal No; choose Apply first Population counts are displayed as people . For example, a game-file size of 1.25 is shown as 1,250 people. New absolute counts are entered as whole people; percentages, multipliers and ratio weights may use decimals. Existing fractional-person data is preserved and shown accurately rather than silently rounded. See Draft and Apply for conversion and precision details. Offline manual Choose About → Manual in the application to open the bundled manual in its own window. Chapters, screenshots, search and themes work without internet access. The bundled version matches the application that supplied it; an older installation therefore keeps its own instructions. Online manual in About and Latest online manual in the bundled manual's footer open the separately hosted site, which may describe a newer version. Compatibility Compatibility with EU5 v1.4.5 is confirmed. The initial supported-game-version setting is 1.4.5 , and can be changed for each installation. This manual setting does not automatically detect the game version or confirm compatibility with later versions. The editor validates the actual data and uses fingerprints to detect changes. This manual covers application 1.0.3 . Europa Universalis V and its content belong to their respective owners. This is an unofficial tool, not affiliated with or endorsed by Paradox Interactive.", "headings": [{"title": "Start here", "url": "index.html#start-here"}, {"title": "What you can edit", "url": "index.html#what-you-can-edit"}, {"title": "Three states, one deliberate workflow", "url": "index.html#three-states-one-deliberate-workflow"}, {"title": "Offline manual", "url": "index.html#offline-manual"}, {"title": "Compatibility", "url": "index.html#compatibility"}]}, {"title": "Install the editor", "label": "Installation", "url": "installation.html", "text": "Choose the package for your operating system. Normal use requires no Python, Node.js, Rust installation or online service. Windows 10 and 11 Download the Windows x86-64 portable ZIP from the project's releases page . Extract the entire archive to its own directory. Open EU5 Population & Demographics Editor.exe at the archive root. Keep the adjacent runtime files and the resources directory together. Moving only the EXE will break the application. There is no launcher script. The application is unsigned; check the release origin and checksum before launching it. GNU/Linux portable Download the Linux x86-64 TAR.GZ and extract it to its own directory. Run the eu5-population-editor binary at the archive root as your ordinary desktop user. Keep all its runtime files together. Read the included RUNNING.txt for distribution-specific library and sandbox requirements. The application uses Electron's Linux desktop libraries and needs glibc; musl-only distributions are not supported. Do not run the editor as root or disable its sandbox to work around an installation problem. Debian and Ubuntu Download the amd64.deb package and install it using your package manager, for example from the directory containing the downloaded file: sudo apt install ./eu5-population-editor_1.0.3_amd64.deb Start the application from the desktop application menu or run eu5-population-editor . The package installs a regular native entry executable in /usr/bin and its runtime in /usr/lib/eu5-population-editor . Check the download Compare the file's SHA-256 hash with the release's SHA256SUMS . On Windows, use PowerShell Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 with the downloaded file. On Linux, use sha256sum with the file. A successful checksum comparison detects a damaged or altered download; obtain both the archive and checksum from the intended release. Updates and removal For portable updates, extract the new archive into a new directory; do not mix runtime files from different versions. Install a new DEB using the package manager. Your settings and map cache live outside the application directory, and your project files remain where you saved them. To remove a portable installation, close the application and remove its extracted directory. Remove a DEB through the package manager. Deleting an installation does not automatically delete saved projects. See Privacy and data for settings and cache locations.", "headings": [{"title": "Windows 10 and 11", "url": "installation.html#windows-10-and-11"}, {"title": "GNU/Linux portable", "url": "installation.html#gnulinux-portable"}, {"title": "Debian and Ubuntu", "url": "installation.html#debian-and-ubuntu"}, {"title": "Check the download", "url": "installation.html#check-the-download"}, {"title": "Updates and removal", "url": "installation.html#updates-and-removal"}]}, {"title": "Create your first project", "label": "Your first project", "url": "first-project.html", "text": "Start with one location and a small change. Review its original, working and draft values before exporting a mod. Load your game data Choose Game Data or Select EU5 game data on the opening screen. Browse to your Europa Universalis V installation directory or its game subdirectory. Wait for loading to finish and read any reported issues. The editor checks the selected folder's contents. A folder name alone does not establish compatibility. Steam library suggestions help locate the game, but you can browse to another installation, including a path containing spaces or non-ASCII characters. You can also open the gear beside About , choose Game , and type or paste the installation path. Save its manual version, then choose Load saved installation . The editor remembers the installation for the next start by default. See Settings to change startup behavior. The 1.0.3 welcome screen fits the window without scrolling. Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ Select a location Choose a country from the alphabetically ordered list, or search by its name or tag. Select a location from the list. The population table shows the demographic groups and the Original , Working and Draft comparisons. You can use All countries / unowned to remove the country filter. The optional map is another way to navigate; it is not required for editing. Make a reviewed edit Choose Edit beside a population group. Select a calculation, such as Increase / decrease percent , and enter 10 for a ten-percent increase. Choose Review draft . Inspect the proposed size, the location total and the differences from Working. Choose Apply to confirm, or Discard to abandon the proposal. Changing a draft input invalidates its previous review. Review it again before applying. A draft is not included in saved projects or exported mods. Save your project Choose Save As and select a location for a .eu5popproj file outside the game installation. Save again after later changes. There is no autosave or crash-recovery feature in this version. Export when ready Choose Validate to review any issues. Then choose Export Mod , complete its fields and select a separate output directory. See Export a mod before using the result with your game. Exporting produces a separate mod; it does not install or enable the mod in the launcher and does not modify the game's original files.", "headings": [{"title": "Load your game data", "url": "first-project.html#load-your-game-data"}, {"title": "Select a location", "url": "first-project.html#select-a-location"}, {"title": "Make a reviewed edit", "url": "first-project.html#make-a-reviewed-edit"}, {"title": "Save your project", "url": "first-project.html#save-your-project"}, {"title": "Export when ready", "url": "first-project.html#export-when-ready"}]}, {"title": "Countries, locations and maps", "label": "Browse the world", "url": "navigation.html", "text": "Use the country and location panels to find a population record. The map provides a visual alternative when compatible map data is available. Countries and locations Countries are sorted by displayed name, with tags retained for identification. Search accepts either a name or a tag. When a readable country name is unavailable, the game identifier remains available. The location list can be searched by name or key. Its filters help focus on modified or problematic records; pagination keeps large worlds manageable. Changing the selection does not apply a draft. Typed forms remain associated with their own location during navigation. Population comparisons The comparison table joins groups from the available states, including groups added or removed by an edit. Review the location total as well as individual rows. Demographic filters change what you inspect; they do not themselves modify the population. Original is the currently loaded source baseline. Working reflects confirmed changes. Draft shows a reviewed pending proposal when one exists. Index the map Choose Index map when compatible map files are present. The first pass reads the location raster and builds a cache. Progress appears while indexing runs. Later selections reuse the index instead of scanning the whole world again. The map supports dragging, scrolling, zoom buttons, arrow-key navigation when focused, and Center . Clicking an editable location selects its record. The selected location is highlighted. Map colors and editable locations Region Appearance Population editing Ordinary land Country color; selected location highlighted Available where the location data supports it Water, including seas and lakes Blue Not editable Corridors and impassable land Dark grey Not editable These classifications come from the loaded game definitions. Water takes precedence if a region also carries a corridor or impassable classification. You can still drag and zoom over noneditable regions, and their location borders remain visible. Clicking water, a corridor or impassable land does not select it as a population-editing target. An ordinary land location is not blocked just because it is unowned or has no current population. The geographic classification determines these restrictions, rather than the absence of an owner or a zero population total. The actual editor's map panel using synthetic demonstration data and an illustrative map. The selected fictional location is highlighted. Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ A second selection in the same synthetic demonstration world. These screenshots illustrate navigation and highlighting; your installed game provides the real geography during normal use. Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ Map limitations Missing or unsupported map resources can disable the map without preventing population editing. Shared or ambiguous RGB identifiers are reported rather than resolved by guesswork. Country colors can use deterministic fallback colors where reliable game colors are unavailable. The cache is stored in the editor's user-data directory and is keyed to the map and definitions. Game files are not modified. See Settings and shortcuts for the directory path.", "headings": [{"title": "Countries and locations", "url": "navigation.html#countries-and-locations"}, {"title": "Population comparisons", "url": "navigation.html#population-comparisons"}, {"title": "Index the map", "url": "navigation.html#index-the-map"}, {"title": "Map colors and editable locations", "url": "navigation.html#map-colors-and-editable-locations"}, {"title": "Map limitations", "url": "navigation.html#map-limitations"}]}, {"title": "Edit population values", "label": "Draft and Apply", "url": "editing.html", "text": "Edits are proposed as drafts and become part of your project only when you choose Apply. This gives you an explicit chance to check exact values and totals. Choose an operation Operation Purpose Exact demographic tuple Change one population group's size Scale population total Change a location's total while distributing its population Add population group Add a group using known type, culture and religion identifiers Remove population group Remove the selected group Rebalance proportions Redistribute a demographic axis while preserving the total A demographic tuple identifies a population group by type, culture and religion. Additional source fields are handled conservatively; invalid or ambiguous data is reported. Population units Population sizes and totals in the interface are counts of people . One source size unit in an EU5 population file represents 1,000 people: source 0.001 means 1 person, and source 1.25 means 1,250 people. Projects and exported EU5 files retain the game's source units; the interface performs the exact conversion. Enter whole numbers for an exact size, a target total, an absolute change or a new group's size. Do not add thousands separators to an input. Percentage changes, multiplication factors and ratio weights can use decimals. New population allocations use one-person steps, while total-preserving operations keep the chosen total exact. Older projects or game data can contain a fraction of a person at their recorded precision. Opening, displaying, saving or exporting that data preserves it. The interface shows the fractional value accurately. If a total-preserving operation requires a whole-person total, explicitly use Set exact value / target to choose that total first and review the change. No implicit conversion rewrites existing data. Calculations Calculation Example input Effect Set exact value / target 120000 Set the selected size or total to 120,000 people Add / subtract absolute value -5000 Subtract 5,000 people Increase / decrease percent 10 Increase by 10 percent Multiply by factor 1.25 Multiply by 1.25 Use a decimal point for rates and weights. Negative changes must not result in invalid negative population sizes. The editor validates the proposal before it can be applied. Percentage and multiplication calculations round the resulting nonnegative target to the nearest person, with an exact half-person rounded upward. Distribution across groups uses deterministic largest-remainder allocation so the displayed target and allocated total agree. Review the resulting rows before applying. Review, Apply and Discard Review draft calculates a proposal. Compare the Original, Working and Draft totals and the change from Working. Apply confirms the current reviewed proposal. Discard removes the pending form and proposal. If you edit an input after reviewing, the proposal is no longer current. Review again before applying. Save and export continue to use only Working values, even while a draft is visible. Add or remove groups For an added group, choose one population type, culture and religion from the definitions loaded with the selected game installation. This creates that exact combination with the size you enter; it does not distribute the size across other combinations. Arbitrary new identifiers cannot be created here. If the combination already exists, use its Edit button instead. Specify a valid size and review the result. When Rebalance ratios introduces a demographic group, it uses the location's positive population profile. If that group's rows were retained at zero, a positive share is divided evenly across those rows instead; their identities and additional fields are kept, but zero values cannot recover previous proportions. This is the rebalance operation's Default preservation behavior. If the whole location is empty, add a positive population group before asking the editor to redistribute it. Removing a group can change the location total. If your intention is to redistribute its share while preserving the total, use Rebalance proportions instead. Undo and redo Undo and redo operate on confirmed actions. Resolve pending drafts first by applying or discarding them. A game-data migration establishes a new source boundary and clears the previous undo history. Save a separate project copy before undertaking a major migration.", "headings": [{"title": "Choose an operation", "url": "editing.html#choose-an-operation"}, {"title": "Population units", "url": "editing.html#population-units"}, {"title": "Calculations", "url": "editing.html#calculations"}, {"title": "Review, Apply and Discard", "url": "editing.html#review-apply-and-discard"}, {"title": "Add or remove groups", "url": "editing.html#add-or-remove-groups"}, {"title": "Undo and redo", "url": "editing.html#undo-and-redo"}]}, {"title": "Rebalance demographics", "label": "Ratios and totals", "url": "ratios.html", "text": "Use Rebalance proportions to change demographic shares without changing the location's total. Use Scale Population separately when the total itself should change. Enter weights Choose Rebalance ratios , then select the culture, religion or population-type axis. Enter one known identifier and weight on each line: identifier_one = 7 identifier_two = 3 These identifiers are placeholders, not game identifiers. Replace them with names present in your loaded definitions. Weights of 7 and 3 request shares of 70 percent and 30 percent; the editor normalizes them. Weights of 70 and 30 express the same proportions. Omitted groups receive zero on the selected axis. Include every group you intend to retain. Repeated identifiers, malformed lines and unknown definitions are rejected. The normalized shares are shown before review. Use Review draft to inspect the actual population rows and exact total. Default preservation The default mode preserves each existing group's internal demographic structure where possible. A newly introduced group uses the location's profile. For example, changing culture shares does not require you to manually recreate every religion and population-type combination. If a retained group's rows are all zero, those rows no longer encode their earlier proportions. Restoring a positive share divides people evenly across the retained rows, using deterministic one-person remainder allocation; their demographic identities and additional fields are preserved. This does not restore historical ratios that were lost when the sizes became zero. Advanced preservation Expand Advanced preservation to preserve each complementary demographic bucket independently. If culture is the edited axis, the complementary buckets are combinations of religion and population type. This option redistributes the chosen axis inside each bucket. Allocation in whole-person steps can produce small differences from the requested overall percentages while each preserved total remains exact. Compare the resulting rows before applying. Legacy data with a fractional-person location total cannot be rebalanced into whole people while preserving that same total. Advanced preservation also requires whole-person totals for every preserved bucket. The editor reports these cases. Choose an explicit whole-person target with Scale Population → Set exact value / target , review the resulting distribution, and then retry the rebalance. It does not silently round the old totals. Change the total separately Choose Scale Population for an exact target, absolute change, percentage change or multiplier. For example, a multiplier of 1.1 requests a ten-percent increase in total population. Review the proposed total and distribution, then apply it. The application uses exact arithmetic and deterministic allocation, with population shown in people. For example, a 10000 target means 10,000 people and is stored as source size 10 in the game file. Existing project and game-file values retain their exact source-unit representation. See Population units for the treatment of legacy fractions.", "headings": [{"title": "Enter weights", "url": "ratios.html#enter-weights"}, {"title": "Default preservation", "url": "ratios.html#default-preservation"}, {"title": "Advanced preservation", "url": "ratios.html#advanced-preservation"}, {"title": "Change the total separately", "url": "ratios.html#change-the-total-separately"}]}, {"title": "Save and reopen projects", "label": "Save and reopen", "url": "projects.html", "text": "A project records your confirmed population edits and the source information needed to detect incompatibilities. Saving a project is separate from exporting a mod. Save and Save As Save writes the current project's confirmed Working state. Save As lets you choose another path and is useful for keeping checkpoints before larger edits. The native file extension is .eu5popproj . The file includes source fingerprints, baselines for modified locations and the information used during migration. It does not bundle a complete game installation. Pending drafts are excluded. Review and apply any draft you want to keep before saving. There is no autosave or crash-recovery system; save regularly. Reopen a project Load the intended Game Data first, then choose Open and select the project. The editor verifies the project's structure and checksum. If the installed source data differs from the recorded baseline, editing and export are blocked until the difference is resolved through migration. Do not manually edit the project JSON to bypass its checksum or source checks. Restore a known-good backup if the file is damaged. New projects and replacing work New Project starts a clean project against the loaded game data. Loading another project or replacing current work can discard unsaved changes and drafts. The application prompts when current work needs attention; save a checkpoint before proceeding. The editor uses a themed confirmation dialog when replacing unsaved work, exiting or restarting. Cancel keeps the current session open. Discard continues without saving. Save Working writes the confirmed changes before continuing; pending Draft edits are still excluded. If you want to keep a draft, cancel and Apply it first. The 1.0.3 unsaved-work dialog, shown with synthetic demonstration data, distinguishes confirmed Working changes from pending Draft edits. Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ File compatibility The public application's version number and the project format version are separate. Version 1.0.3 uses native project format 1. A project remains subject to schema, checksum and game-source checks regardless of the application version recorded in it. Project sizes remain in EU5 source units; the person-count interface does not reinterpret existing saved values or discard fractional precision. See Import existing population edits for supported older JSON projects and mod population files. See Migrate after a game update when the game data has changed.", "headings": [{"title": "Save and Save As", "url": "projects.html#save-and-save-as"}, {"title": "Reopen a project", "url": "projects.html#reopen-a-project"}, {"title": "New projects and replacing work", "url": "projects.html#new-projects-and-replacing-work"}, {"title": "File compatibility", "url": "projects.html#file-compatibility"}]}, {"title": "Export a population mod", "label": "Export a mod", "url": "export.html", "text": "Export Mod creates a separate mod from confirmed Working changes. Exporting does not change the original installation or enable the mod in the game launcher. Before exporting Apply all drafts you intend to include, save your project, and choose Validate . Read any issues and resolve problems that block compatibility or export. Select a destination outside the game installation. Export fields Field What to enter Name A readable title for your mod ID A stable lowercase, folder-safe identifier Version Your mod's own version, such as 1.0.0 Supported EU5 version The saved manual version for the loaded installation; change it through Game settings Optional ZIP Whether to also create a ZIP of the generated mod Compatibility with EU5 v1.4.5 is confirmed, and the initial supported-game-version value is 1.4.5 . Supported EU5 version displays the saved value. Use Game settings to open Settings, select the loaded installation, edit Manual EU5 version , and choose Save settings . A leading v is accepted and normalized when saved. The setting is remembered per installation without requiring an export. This field is manual metadata. Changing it does not make incompatible game files compatible and does not replace a migration after an actual data change. Write the mod With 1.4.5 selected, no unverified-version acknowledgment is required. If you select another game version, acknowledge that compatibility with that selected version has not been verified before exporting. Choose Choose output and export and select the destination. The editor stages the output, reparses the population data and verifies the result before completing the export. When Remember project and export folders is enabled in Settings, the output picker starts in the last export destination. That location is remembered separately from project folders. An existing export directory is not recursively overwritten. Choose a new destination or move an older export after verifying what it contains. What is produced The output contains the changed population source files with unchanged location content retained, .metadata/metadata.json , change reports, and the optional ZIP. Source UTF-8 BOM and line endings are preserved where applicable. Legacy descriptor files are not generated. Change reports can also be exported individually as CSV, JSON or Markdown from the dialog. Human-readable population reports use person counts. JSON reports retain their raw source-unit values and include explicitly named person-count fields, so existing integrations can distinguish the two representations. The exported EU5 population files continue to use the game's thousand-person source units. Displaying people does not change the file format or round untouched source data. Use the exported mod Follow the mod-loading process supported by your installed game and launcher. EU5 v1.4.5 compatibility is confirmed. Keep a backup of your project; later game versions, changes to launcher conventions and interactions with other enabled mods require their own verification. Do not copy the generated population files over your vanilla installation. Keep exported output separate from the source files used for editing and migration.", "headings": [{"title": "Before exporting", "url": "export.html#before-exporting"}, {"title": "Export fields", "url": "export.html#export-fields"}, {"title": "Write the mod", "url": "export.html#write-the-mod"}, {"title": "What is produced", "url": "export.html#what-is-produced"}, {"title": "Use the exported mod", "url": "export.html#use-the-exported-mod"}]}, {"title": "Migrate after a game update", "label": "Upgrade a project", "url": "migration.html", "text": "When a game update changes the population source data, use Upgrade Project / Mod to review how your confirmed edits should carry forward. The editor does not silently replace incompatible data. Prepare Save a backup of the project and resolve any pending drafts. Open the project, choose Upgrade Project / Mod , and select the new vanilla game installation. Planning compares the recorded old baseline with your modifications and the new source; it does not change Working until you commit. Pending work includes a typed editing form that has not yet been reviewed, as well as a reviewed Draft. Apply or discard it before starting migration. A form from the old data is not carried into the new source generation. A saved project can be opened for migration when its source fingerprint differs from the installed game, because it contains the old baseline. Normal editing and export remain blocked until the source difference is resolved. A remembered selection is cleared if the location is no longer editable. Understand the three versions Label Meaning Old vanilla (OV) The saved baseline for the modified location Old modified (M) Your confirmed Working changes New vanilla (NV) The newly loaded game data Read the rows, totals, additional fields and proposed result for each item. Row reordering alone does not create a conflict. Classifications Classification Proposed behavior Safe — unchanged vanilla Carry your confirmed changes forward unchanged Safe — scaled to new vanilla Scale the modified total by the new/old vanilla total ratio when the identity and other compatibility checks allow it Conflict — manual review required Require an explicit resolution for changed identities, unknown fields or incompatible source semantics Conflict — removed location Acknowledge removal; the editor does not recreate the missing location Conflict — restricted location Explicitly choose Keep new vanilla when the new game classifies the location as sea, lake, corridor or impassable land Scaling uses the whole-location total ratio when compatible identity sets match. It does not assume every new vanilla group changed by the same percentage. Resolve conflicts The available resolutions include keeping new vanilla, applying the old modification, and applying the scaled old modification where valid. Inspect the proposed rows after resolving. Removed locations require their own explicit acknowledgment. For a restricted location, Keep new vanilla is the only resolution. Old or scaled population edits cannot be applied to water, corridors or impassable land. The migration report retains the old edits for review; they are not silently discarded while planning. Directly applying old ratios onto new vanilla and opening a conflict in a temporary normal editing workspace are not available in 1.0.3. You can keep new vanilla, commit the reviewed plan and then edit the location normally if it remains ordinary land eligible for population editing. Review world changes Inspect added and removed locations, ownership changes and possible replacement country tags. New locations retain new vanilla data. Tag candidates are suggestions; even one candidate requires an explicit choice. No replacement is silently inferred. Acknowledge that you reviewed the topology and tag changes before committing. A tag mapping updates remembered country selection; population migration remains keyed by location. Commit and save Export the migration report as JSON or Markdown if you want a record before committing. Commit migration remains unavailable until required resolutions and review acknowledgments are complete. Committing switches the project to the new source and clears undo history at that source boundary. Save the resulting project separately after commit. Closing the migration dialog without committing leaves the old Working project active.", "headings": [{"title": "Prepare", "url": "migration.html#prepare"}, {"title": "Understand the three versions", "url": "migration.html#understand-the-three-versions"}, {"title": "Classifications", "url": "migration.html#classifications"}, {"title": "Resolve conflicts", "url": "migration.html#resolve-conflicts"}, {"title": "Review world changes", "url": "migration.html#review-world-changes"}, {"title": "Commit and save", "url": "migration.html#commit-and-save"}]}, {"title": "Import existing population edits", "label": "Import an existing mod", "url": "import.html", "text": "The editor can propose conversion of supported older project JSON and existing mod population files. Correct baseline data is essential to distinguish your changes from the original game content. Import a mod population source Load the old vanilla game data that the mod was based on. Choose Upgrade Project / Mod , then Import mod population source . Select the mod's replacement population source file. Review the conversion proposal and unresolved records. Confirm that the selected source is the correct baseline before committing the conversion. Import compares the mod file against corresponding vanilla population data. It does not import every feature of an arbitrary mod. Non-population changes, missing locations and invalid identifiers remain unresolved and block conversion rather than being silently skipped. Import older project JSON Supported legacy JSON uses the eu5-pop-editor-project format. Open the file against the matching old vanilla source, review the conversion proposal and confirm the baseline. Unsupported layouts or unresolved records must be addressed before conversion can be committed. Keep the original file as a backup. Once conversion succeeds, use Save As to write a native .eu5popproj file. Move to newer game data After conversion against the correct old baseline, use Upgrade Project / Mod to select the new vanilla game data. Review the separate migration plan using the normal migration workflow . Do not start by loading new vanilla and treating it as the old mod's baseline. That can misrepresent game changes as your own modifications.", "headings": [{"title": "Import a mod population source", "url": "import.html#import-a-mod-population-source"}, {"title": "Import older project JSON", "url": "import.html#import-older-project-json"}, {"title": "Move to newer game data", "url": "import.html#move-to-newer-game-data"}]}, {"title": "Check your changes", "label": "Validation and reports", "url": "validation.html", "text": "Validation checks the project's data and compatibility before export. Reports help you inspect confirmed changes and migration decisions outside the editor. Validate the project Choose Validate to see the current state and listed issues. Each issue identifies the affected source or location, describes the problem and provides a suggested resolution. A source fingerprint mismatch indicates that the loaded game data differs from the project baseline. Use migration instead of changing a version label to bypass the mismatch. Invalid demographic identifiers must be resolved against the loaded definitions. Optional localization and map problems can disable those features while leaving population editing available. Read the affected-feature details rather than assuming every warning has the same effect. Change reports Open Export Mod and choose CSV report , JSON report or Markdown report . These reports describe confirmed Working changes. They do not turn pending drafts into confirmed edits. CSV is convenient for tables; JSON retains structured data; Markdown is useful for a readable change record. Save reports outside the game installation. CSV and Markdown population values are labelled as people. JSON retains the raw source-unit values and provides separate person-count fields. A game-file source value of 1 represents 1,000 people; do not treat the two report fields as interchangeable. Existing fractional precision remains visible where present. Migration reports Within a migration plan, export JSON or Markdown before committing. The report records the old baseline, modified and new rows, totals, scaling ratio, proposal and explicit resolutions. A report is not a saved project or an exportable mod. Keep the native project if you intend to continue editing. Limits of a successful check Compatibility with EU5 v1.4.5 is confirmed separately from the editor's automated validation. Validation checks the loaded data, and export additionally reparses and verifies generated population sources. These automated checks do not run the game. Later game versions and interactions with other mods require separate verification.", "headings": [{"title": "Validate the project", "url": "validation.html#validate-the-project"}, {"title": "Change reports", "url": "validation.html#change-reports"}, {"title": "Migration reports", "url": "validation.html#migration-reports"}, {"title": "Limits of a successful check", "url": "validation.html#limits-of-a-successful-check"}]}, {"title": "Settings and keyboard shortcuts", "label": "Settings and shortcuts", "url": "settings.html", "text": "Open Settings using the gear immediately to the right of About . Its three sections bring appearance, game data and window preferences together. Settings are stored locally, outside the application directory. Appearance Choose System , Light or Dark . System follows your operating system's appearance and is the default. The preview changes immediately; choose Save settings to keep it, or Cancel to return to the saved appearance. The Appearance section previews the selected theme before saving. Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ The in-app manual follows Settings → Appearance , including live previews and cancellation. It has no separate theme selector. The hosted manual has its own theme button, which cycles through system, light and dark independently of the application. Game installation and version In Game , type or paste the Installation directory , or use Browse… to choose it with the operating system's folder picker. Select the Europa Universalis V installation root or its game subdirectory. Both refer to the same installation preference. Enter the Manual EU5 version shown by the game, then choose Save settings . The initial value is 1.4.5 . Versions are remembered separately for each installation; a leading v is accepted and normalized when saved. The editor does not detect the version automatically. Keep an installation and its manual version together. Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ Use Load saved installation to load the saved data. Replacing an active session still asks you to resolve unsaved work. Saving settings alone does not replace the current project. Load saved game data on startup is enabled by default. Disable it to open the welcome screen without loading an installation. The welcome screen offers Load saved game data when a directory has been saved. Startup loading prepares game data; it does not reopen a project file automatically. If the saved installation cannot be loaded, the editor reports the problem and leaves the welcome screen available so you can correct the directory in Settings. Remember project and export folders is enabled by default. The editor remembers the last project folder and export destination separately, so choosing an export destination does not change the next project dialog's starting folder. Turn the option off to stop remembering these locations. Export uses the saved manual version for the loaded installation. Compatibility with EU5 v1.4.5 is confirmed; changing a version label does not confirm compatibility with another release. If the actual game data changes, use project migration rather than changing the version to bypass a fingerprint mismatch. Window placement In Window , Remember window size and position is enabled by default. It restores the normal window size and position, as well as whether the editor was maximized. Turn it off and save to clear the recorded placement and use the default centered window at the next start. Window restoration can be disabled or reset independently of game data. Open full-size screenshot ↗ Open full-size screenshot ↗ Choose Reset window layout , then Save settings , to return to the default size and center the editor. If the display arrangement has changed, saved placement is adjusted to keep the window on an available display. The welcome screen fits the available space without scrolling; working panels and Settings can scroll within their own areas when needed. User-data locations Operating system Default application directory Windows %AppData%\\eu5-population-editor GNU/Linux ~/.config/eu5-population-editor The application stores preferences, remembered game versions and its map cache here. On Linux, a custom XDG configuration directory can change the base location. Your .eu5popproj files and exported mods are stored in the locations you explicitly choose. Back them up separately. Close the application before manually moving or removing its settings/cache directory. Application shortcuts Shortcut Action Ctrl+S Save confirmed Working changes Ctrl+Shift+S Save As Ctrl+O Open a project Arrow keys while the map is focused Pan the map Resolve pending drafts before undoing or redoing confirmed changes. The toolbar shows whether actions are available for the current state. Manual shortcuts About → Manual opens the edition bundled with your installed application. Its search and illustrations run locally, and its appearance follows the application. Close its window to return to the editor; closing the manual does not change the current project. Online manual in About and Latest online manual in the bundled manual's footer open the hosted site separately. The manual window menu provides Home ( Alt+Home ), Back ( Alt+Left ), Forward ( Alt+Right ) and zoom controls. Opening a full-size screenshot stays in the manual window; use Back to return to its chapter. Opening Manual again focuses the existing window. Closing the editor also closes its manual window. Press / or Ctrl+K to open manual search. Use the arrow keys to move through results, Enter to follow a result and Escape to close the dialog. Search runs locally in your browser, including when the built manual is opened from disk.", "headings": [{"title": "Appearance", "url": "settings.html#appearance"}, {"title": "Game installation and version", "url": "settings.html#game-installation-and-version"}, {"title": "Window placement", "url": "settings.html#window-placement"}, {"title": "User-data locations", "url": "settings.html#user-data-locations"}, {"title": "Application shortcuts", "url": "settings.html#application-shortcuts"}, {"title": "Manual shortcuts", "url": "settings.html#manual-shortcuts"}]}, {"title": "Troubleshooting", "label": "Troubleshooting", "url": "troubleshooting.html", "text": "Start with the message shown in the editor. Keep a backup of your project, and do not modify the vanilla installation while diagnosing a problem. The application does not start For portable builds, confirm that you extracted the whole archive and retained its runtime files. Use the package for your operating system and x86-64 architecture. On Linux, review RUNNING.txt and the package manager's dependency messages. Launch as an ordinary desktop user. If the downloaded file's SHA-256 does not match the release manifest, download it again. Do not mix files from two application versions. Game Data is rejected Select the actual installation or game directory, not a saved project or exported mod. Confirm that the expected setup and definitions are present and readable. Steam Browse can help locate the installation. The editor follows active setup bookmarks and safe nested includes. Multiple active start dates select the earliest with a warning; ambiguous equal-date selections and conflicting included sources are rejected. Unusual or changed data layouts may require an editor update. The saved installation does not load at startup Open Settings → Game and check the installation directory. A moved installation, disconnected drive or missing files can prevent startup loading. Correct the path, save it and choose Load saved installation . Leave Load saved game data on startup enabled to try that installation automatically next time, or disable it to start at the welcome screen. Saving a different installation in Settings does not replace the currently loaded data. Load the saved installation when you are ready to switch; the normal unsaved-work confirmation still applies. Window placement or appearance is unexpected Use Settings → Window → Reset window layout , then Save settings , to restore the default centered layout. Turn off Remember window size and position if each launch should use that default. Restored windows are fitted to an available display when the monitor arrangement changes. Use Settings → Appearance for System , Light or Dark . A theme preview is kept only after Save settings ; Cancel restores the saved theme. The in-app manual follows these changes. The hosted manual's theme button is independent of the application. Country names or the map are missing Population editing can continue when optional English localization or map data is unavailable. Identifiers remain usable. Load matching map files and choose Index map if the control is available. Ambiguous shared map colors are reported rather than guessed. A map region cannot be selected for editing Blue water and lakes, and dark-grey corridors or impassable land, are not editable population locations. Dragging, zooming and location borders remain available over those regions. Ordinary unowned or empty land is not blocked solely because of ownership or population totals. Apply is disabled Choose Review draft after the most recent input change. Resolve any invalid value or unknown identifier. A proposal must match the current inputs before Apply becomes available. Saved changes appear to be missing Save and export include only confirmed Working values. Pending drafts are excluded. Verify that you applied the proposal and saved the intended project file. There is no autosave in 1.0.3. A legacy population total is not a whole number Existing source data can represent a fraction of a person. The editor displays and preserves it. A rebalance cannot preserve a fractional total using only whole-person allocations; advanced preservation also checks each complementary bucket. Choose Scale Population → Set exact value / target to explicitly set a whole-person total, review and apply the result, then retry. See Population units . The online manual describes a different version Use Manual in the installed application to read its bundled edition. The online site can describe a newer release. Offline chapters, search and images are included with the application and do not need Python or a local server. If bundled files are missing or fail their version check, extract the complete application archive into a clean directory instead of mixing resources from different releases. Source mismatch after a game update Use Upgrade Project / Mod and select the new vanilla data. Changing Supported EU5 version affects metadata only and does not make the recorded source fingerprint match. Migration cannot be committed Resolve every item without a proposal and acknowledge review of new/removed locations, ownership changes and country-tag candidates. Inspect errors for invalid choices or changed source files. Removed modified locations require explicit removal acknowledgment. Export is blocked Check validation, the export fields and the supported game-version value. For a selected version other than 1.4.5, also acknowledge that compatibility with that version has not been verified. Choose a writable output directory outside the game installation. The editor will not recursively overwrite an existing export directory. Report a reproducible issue Use the application issue tracker . Include the application version, operating system, game version, action taken and exact error. Remove personal paths and details from reports or screenshots. Do not upload proprietary game files; use a minimal synthetic example where possible. For incorrect instructions or a broken manual page, use the manual issue tracker .", "headings": [{"title": "The application does not start", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-application-does-not-start"}, {"title": "Game Data is rejected", "url": "troubleshooting.html#game-data-is-rejected"}, {"title": "The saved installation does not load at startup", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-saved-installation-does-not-load-at-startup"}, {"title": "Window placement or appearance is unexpected", "url": "troubleshooting.html#window-placement-or-appearance-is-unexpected"}, {"title": "Country names or the map are missing", "url": "troubleshooting.html#country-names-or-the-map-are-missing"}, {"title": "A map region cannot be selected for editing", "url": "troubleshooting.html#a-map-region-cannot-be-selected-for-editing"}, {"title": "Apply is disabled", "url": "troubleshooting.html#apply-is-disabled"}, {"title": "Saved changes appear to be missing", "url": "troubleshooting.html#saved-changes-appear-to-be-missing"}, {"title": "A legacy population total is not a whole number", "url": "troubleshooting.html#a-legacy-population-total-is-not-a-whole-number"}, {"title": "The online manual describes a different version", "url": "troubleshooting.html#the-online-manual-describes-a-different-version"}, {"title": "Source mismatch after a game update", "url": "troubleshooting.html#source-mismatch-after-a-game-update"}, {"title": "Migration cannot be committed", "url": "troubleshooting.html#migration-cannot-be-committed"}, {"title": "Export is blocked", "url": "troubleshooting.html#export-is-blocked"}, {"title": "Report a reproducible issue", "url": "troubleshooting.html#report-a-reproducible-issue"}]}, {"title": "Privacy, data and licensing", "label": "Privacy and licensing", "url": "privacy.html", "text": "The editor processes game data locally and does not require an account or online editing service. The manual also keeps search queries in your browser. Files and network access The application reads the game installation and creates projects, reports and exported mods only through the documented local workflows. It does not modify vanilla game files. Settings and cache data use the application user-data directory . Normal editing has no telemetry, remote content or background model/data downloads. Manual opens the bundled local edition in a separate application window without a local HTTP server. Choosing an explicit online-manual, repository, download or issue link opens an external website in your browser. That website's own network and privacy behavior applies. The generated manual uses local styles, scripts, images and system fonts. Its browser search is local. The hosted manual stores its chosen color-theme preference; the in-app manual follows the application's appearance setting and stores no separate theme choice. Hosting providers may process ordinary access requests when you visit the hosted site. Backups and sharing Keep backups of native projects, especially before migration. Reports and projects can contain location identifiers, game-derived data and your edits. Inspect them before sharing. Remove personal filesystem paths from issue reports and screenshots. The source repository and release packages do not include proprietary game files. Users supply their own installed game data. An exported mod can include game-derived population content; this software's license does not grant rights over that game content. License and attribution The application's original code and this manual's original code and text are licensed under GNU Affero General Public License version 3 only (AGPL-3.0-only) . The full license is included in each repository and in the built manual . Third-party components retain their own notices and licenses; see Third-party notices . Screenshots in this edition show the actual editor interface using synthetic demonstration data, fictional countries and locations, and an illustrative map. They do not include shipped EU5 game assets. The software and manual licenses do not grant rights over third-party game content that users load separately. Copyright © 2026 davoda1. Europa Universalis V, related trademarks and game content belong to their respective owners. EU5 Population Editor is an unofficial tool and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Paradox Interactive.", "headings": [{"title": "Files and network access", "url": "privacy.html#files-and-network-access"}, {"title": "Backups and sharing", "url": "privacy.html#backups-and-sharing"}, {"title": "License and attribution", "url": "privacy.html#license-and-attribution"}]}, {"title": "Version 1.0.3", "label": "Release notes", "url": "release-notes.html", "text": "Version 1.0.3 adds a bundled offline manual and shows population as people. It supports Windows and GNU/Linux x86-64 and retains project format 1 and the existing Draft, Apply, migration and export workflow. New in 1.0.3 Open the application-matched manual in a separate local window, with offline chapters, screenshots, search and themes. Use the application's Appearance setting for the in-app manual, including previews and cancellation. The hosted manual retains its independent theme selector. Keep the hosted manual available as an explicit online option. Display and enter absolute population counts in people; percentage inputs, multipliers and ratio weights retain decimal support. Allocate new edits in one-person steps while preserving existing project/source values and their fractional precision when untouched. Report fractional legacy totals that cannot be rebalanced into whole people without an explicit total change. During standard rebalancing, use the positive location profile for a newly introduced demographic group, and restore retained zero-sized rows evenly without losing their additional fields. Require pending editing forms to be resolved before migration, including forms that have not yet been reviewed. Show people in human-readable reports while retaining source-unit values alongside labelled people fields in JSON reports. Include editable manual source, frozen generated pages and SHA-256 verification in the application source distribution. New in 1.0.2 Update the desktop runtime to Electron 45.2.1 stable. Build with Node.js 26.13.0 LTS and its bundled npm. Refresh the application screenshots throughout this manual. New in 1.0.1 Keep the welcome screen within the available window space without scrolling. Open Settings using the gear beside About, with Appearance, Game and Window sections. Preview System, Light or Dark appearance before saving; Cancel restores the saved theme. Type, paste or browse for an installation directory and save its manual EU5 version. Load the saved installation at startup, or turn automatic loading off. Restore normal window size, position and maximized state, with a reset option and recovery for changed display arrangements. Remember project and export folders separately, with an option to disable folder history. Show the loaded installation's saved game version in Export Mod and change it through Game settings. Refresh the manual's screenshots and add illustrated Settings and welcome-screen guidance. Features retained from 1.0.0 The first public release introduced the editing, migration and export capabilities below. They remain available in 1.0.3. Editing and review Browse countries alphabetically by name and search by country name or tag. Inspect population groups and Original, Working and Draft comparisons. Edit exact sizes, add or remove known groups, rebalance demographic proportions, and scale totals. Review and explicitly apply proposals; save and export use only confirmed Working state. Undo and redo confirmed changes after resolving pending drafts. Projects and game data Save checksummed .eu5popproj files with source fingerprints and migration baselines. Discover supported active setup layouts and follow confined setup includes. Review migration between old vanilla, modified and new vanilla data. Propose conversion of supported legacy JSON and mod population sources. Index compatible maps once and reuse the local cache. Render water and lakes blue, and corridors and impassable land dark grey, while retaining map navigation and location borders. Restrict population editing by geographic classification, with water precedence; ordinary unowned or empty land remains eligible. Remember the manually entered game version per installation, initially 1.4.5 . Export and packaging Export source-preserving population mods with metadata, reports and an optional ZIP. Use confirmed EU5 v1.4.5 compatibility; exporting for another selected game version requires an explicit unverified-version acknowledgment. Export change reports in CSV, JSON or Markdown and migration reports in JSON or Markdown. Provide Windows portable ZIP, Linux portable TAR.GZ and Debian packages with native entry executables. License original application and manual work under AGPL-3.0-only. Current limits There is no autosave, save-game editing, automatic game-version detection or in-game mod activation. Confirmed v1.4.5 compatibility does not automatically extend to later game versions or combinations with other mods. Map and localization availability depend on the selected game data. See the application repository's testing report for the verification performed for a particular build. Download availability and completed CI status are shown on the application repository .", "headings": [{"title": "New in 1.0.3", "url": "release-notes.html#new-in-103"}, {"title": "New in 1.0.2", "url": "release-notes.html#new-in-102"}, {"title": "New in 1.0.1", "url": "release-notes.html#new-in-101"}, {"title": "Features retained from 1.0.0", "url": "release-notes.html#features-retained-from-100"}, {"title": "Editing and review", "url": "release-notes.html#editing-and-review"}, {"title": "Projects and game data", "url": "release-notes.html#projects-and-game-data"}, {"title": "Export and packaging", "url": "release-notes.html#export-and-packaging"}, {"title": "Current limits", "url": "release-notes.html#current-limits"}]}];
